I hear America singing—the varied carols I hear:  
those of mechanics, each singing his own, as it should be, blithe and strong;  
the carpenter singing his as he measures his plank or beam;  
the mason singing his as he prepares for work or leaves off work;  
the boatman singing what belongs to him in his boat, and the deckhand singing on the steamboat deck;  
the shoemaker singing as he sits at his bench, and the hatter singing as he stands;  
the woodcutter’s song, and the ploughboy’s on his way in the morning, at the noon intermission, or at sundown;  
the delightful singing of the mother, the young wife at work, or the girl sewing or washing—  
each singing what belongs to him or her, and to no one else.  
The day sings what belongs to the day; at night, a gathering of young fellows, robust and friendly,  
singing with open mouths their strong, melodious songs.
